it's been two weeks since the house  passed the sweeping voting rights  legislation known as the for the people  act  or hr1 and now that bill has been  formally introduced in the senate the  first hearing on the bill is set for  wednesday  it includes provisions like automatic  voter registration same day registration  at least 15 days of early voting  expanded mail-in voting  it also restores voting rights to the  formerly incarcerated  banned gerrymandered district maps  limits dark money and politics  and here's a biggie makes election day a  federal holiday  among other many reforms republicans are  fervently against this bill calling it  federal overreach and an  unconstitutional power grab by democrats  some even falsely suggest it would allow  for illegal voting  they're being very proportionate in  their criticisms of the bill  here's gop senator mike lee on fox news  everything about this bill is rotten to  the core this is a bill as if  written in hell by the devil himself  okay then although the bill has already  passed in the house twice in fact its  chances in the senate with the likes of  mike lee  are pretty slim especially with a  filibuster in place  but with republicans trying to restrict  voting rights at the state level across  the country  activists and even celebrities are  urging congress to do whatever it takes  to pass the for the people act among  them is the outspoken actor and activist  piper perabo who you may know from  movies like coyote ugly or tv shows like  covert affairs  i spoke with her earlier today  piper perabo thank you so much for  joining me on the show  thanks so much for having me  you've been very outspoken about voting  rights in recent weeks and getting  people registered to vote  why is the for the people act hr1 in the  house and now s1  in the senate this week why is it so  important that passes into law asap in  your view well  sr1 which was introduced today would  make  voting more easy and accessible for all  eligible americans and just recently we  had the 56th anniversary  of the march across edmund pettus bridge  for voting rights  and i can't believe that it's been 56  years and we're still  fighting for equality at the voting box  that's longer than i've been alive so i  just  am so hopeful that we can pass this bill  so that every  eligible american has access to the  ballot  yes and we've been talking a great deal  about the bill and its contents on this  show both today and on previous weeks it  is so important  of course there's a lot of resistance to  it the republicans say this bill is  federal government overreach it's just a  naked power grab by democrats what do  you say to them  well i would say that americans don't  have to listen to republicans or  democrats on this bill they can read it  for themselves you can go  to congress.gov and read the entire bill  for yourself so that you know exactly  what's in it  and you don't have to fall for any  headlines or clickbait or  sound bites that politicians tell you  what this bill is about is about  automatic voter registration for all  eligible americans  making sure that there's early access to  the ballot at least 15 days in every  state including a weekend  and time before 9 a.m and after 5 p.m  and mail-in  and mail-in voting which we all saw was  so important during the pandemic so  these are kind of common sense voting uh  rights  that make access to the ballot equal for  everyone  and so it's not really a republican or a  democratic bill i don't understand why  people speak out against it  yeah it's a good point the idea of  having an election day be a federal  holiday that's not a  i'm pretty sure democrats and republican  voters would benefit from that  piper we know this bill has a tough path  ahead especially in the senate but in  the meantime  the brennan center so far identified  more than 250  restrictive voting bills in 43 states  uh a lot of the state legislatures are  run by republicans they have a good  chance of getting those bills through  if hr one doesn't happen you know it's  going to be even more voter suppression  how do activists and organizers like  yourself  how do you fight these restrictions on  the ground in at the state level  i think it's really important that  people understand who their state  representatives are you know especially  on the news at night we talk about  congress and the senate and the  president  and the federal level is important but  they can limit your voting rights at the  state level as well  so especially in these 43 different  states where they're trying to limit  your access to the ballot  voters need to be calling their state  legislatures  and saying they don't want these kind of  bills and i think  legislators really respond to those  kinds of things because especially the  state level they're not used to being  inundated with calls from voters  and they really listen if you're a  constituent they want to hear from you  so talking about uh inundating people  with calls and being an  activist a lot of people watching this  will really only know you from your tv  shows or your movies  have you always been an activist you've  always been politically active  since what age what attracted you to it  i haven't always been politically active  in fact i think part of the reason why  i'm so active now is that i realized i  wasn't paying attention  when trump made those comments on the  access hollywood  bus that were recorded about women i was  so shocked that someone like that  could be running for president of the  united states and i thought for sure his  candidacy would be over as soon as that  was released  and when he became president i realized  that i hadn't been paying attention and  i needed to  pay a lot more attention so i've been  really busy the last year and a half  years  i think really busy is an understatement  and you did a lot of catching up  uh in terms of protests i think we've  got pictures here that we could see you  were actually arrested  after disrupting then supreme court  nominee brett kavanaugh's confirmation  hearing  back in 2018. this week  senator sheldon whitehouse asked  attorney general merrick garland  to look into the fbi background check of  kavanaugh at the time  of that of those hearings and  there's this argument he's putting for  there you are i was just waiting for you  to pop up  the there's this argument that the  accusations of sexual assault against it  were not  properly investigated uh he even went as  far as suggesting the fbi probe was fake  given you were so outspoken at the time  against kavanaugh what do you make of  these allegations now and this idea that  kavanaugh's just sitting on the bench  doling out opinions like any other  justice  well i was opposed to brett kavanaugh as  a supreme court justice  before dr ford came out with her  allegations so i was opposed to him  because of his views against freedom of  choice  and also because at the time the  president was an unindicted  co-conspirator in a felony investigation  and so i didn't think was appropriate  that he be appointing judges at that  time  and then after dr ford came out there  were a lot of concerns about what the  fbi  did to investigate kavanaugh's  background and so i really applaud  senator whitehouse  for coming out on this and asking for  that to be investigated  and if everything was done properly and  is on the up and up then  there's nothing to worry about and if  problems arise they can be investigated  and supreme court justices can be  impeached just like presidents  it's a very good point just like  presidents and president donald trump  got impeached twice  um we just saw sadly horrifically this  week another incident of violence  against women specifically against  asian women in atlanta and we also heard  a police officer basically trying to  explain away the suspect's actions as  quote having a bad day  and suggesting it's not racist because  the suspect said  it wasn't racist um that officer since  apologized for his comments  but it's still kind of indicative of the  underlying misogyny and racism that's  pervasive  in our society that not only leads to  crimes like this but people  offering excuses for especially white  male perpetrators of such crimes  yeah i think it's on all of us to stand  up and speak out about violence against  asian americans right now and  violence and bigotry anywhere in our  country  another actor friend of mine daniel day  kim was testifying for the house  judiciary committee  just today and one of the things he  talked about was the  hate crimes act which is before the  judiciary committee right now  which would do many things but one of  which would provide a streamlined way to  report hate crimes  and it's kind of surprising that in a  country with a history like ours there  isn't  a national hotline or a way to easily do  that  and so um i'm really grateful that that  bill exists and people  can call their members of congress and  tell them that they want to support  the hate crimes act  yes it's so important and i'm glad uh we  could have you here to talk about that  and so much more but we're out of time  piper parabo thank you so much for  joining me tonight i appreciate it  thank you so much for having me i really  appreciate you taking the time  hi i'm mehdi hussain thanks for checking  out our channel on youtube you can see  more of the mehdi hassan show  by clicking on any of the videos on this  screen and make sure you subscribe below  to stay up to date  on the day's biggest stories thank you  for watching
 

